---
import RwLayout from '../../layouts/RwLayout.astro';
import PromptBox from '../../components/PromptBox.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const pSetup = `Set up this project's instruction file, AGENTS.md (the file of standing, project-level instructions that coding agents load alongside your prompts). Create it if it does not exist, and add the content between the triple quotes:

"""
This repository scores recent weather forecasts against the ERA5 reanalysis.

Conventions:
- All internal computation in the API's native units (°C, km/h, mm). Report tables and figures in °F, mph, and inches. Convert once, at the reporting boundary, never mid-pipeline.
- All times UTC (timezone=GMT at fetch). A displayed time states its zone.
- Raw API responses under data/raw/ are read-only once hashed into PROVENANCE.md. Every claim about what the data says must be checkable against them.
- A null in a response is an explicitly missing value in our records: never zero, never a silently dropped row.

Gates. Stop and ask before:
- deleting or rewriting anything under data/raw/,
- changing a statistic's definition or a tolerance,
- declaring any task complete.
"""`;
---

<RwLayout
  title="Setup — Reference Workflow"
  description="Pick a coding agent and give the project its instruction file with the setup prompt. Model and reasoning-effort settings are optional."
  current="setup"
>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Set up your agent</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-gray-600">
      Pick a coding agent and paste one prompt.
    </p>

    <article class="prose prose-gray max-w-none mt-8">
      <p>
        You need a coding agent: a tool that works directly in a project, designing, writing, and
        running its code, rather than a general-purpose chat window. Claude Code, Codex, Gemini
        CLI, and Cursor are common examples, and most others should work as well. The exercise
        relies on nothing unique to any of them, whether they run in a terminal, an editor, or a
        cloud workspace. If your agent runs from the command line, the only preparation is a new,
        empty directory for the project. The agent installs what the project needs as it goes,
        asking your permission first if your tool is set up that way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many tools can also run the agent's commands in a sandbox that limits what they can reach
        outside the project. If yours offers one, it's worth turning on. Two examples at the time
        of writing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Claude Code: the <code>/sandbox</code> command, inside a running session.</li>
        <li>
          Codex: the <code>--sandbox</code> option when you start a session, as in
          <code>codex --sandbox workspace-write</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two settings live in your tool rather than in any prompt — which model runs, and how much
        reasoning effort it spends — and the defaults work fine. More capable models and higher
        effort generally cost more and take longer per task. If you want a feel for how models
        compare, running this exercise multiple times using different models is a good way to get
        one. In Claude Code these are the
        <code>/model</code> and <code>/effort</code> commands, and Codex and Gemini CLI have the
        same two choices under their own names.
      </p>
      <p>
        The only setup is the project's instruction file, the cheapest structural safeguard there
        is (<a href={`${base}framework/structure/`}>the structure chapter</a> explains why). Paste
        this prompt first:
      </p>

      <PromptBox label="Setup prompt — the project file" text={pSetup} />

      <p>
        The Community Repository carries a fuller worked example of agent configuration.
        <em>(Links land with the Community Repository launch.)</em>
      </p>
    </article>
</RwLayout>
